"Eq" is almost right. Really the answer is "equus",
though. I've got a pet warhorse stranded one level
above, and two large dogs and a large cat all
stranded one level below, all at max HD and "extra
healing"ed to max HP. I don't want them on-level
when I'm altar camping, they get in the way and
compete for corpses, but they'll take even cursed
treats when I stop by to re-nourish them
occasionally, and veggie treats like carrots are
scarce enough (those are from a (knight's(?)
Hearsed bones pile) to make any you find worth
keeping to feed to vegetarian pets, even cursed
ones, horses being so hard to keep fed anyway.

> ...with all this fancy enchanted equipment...

Sigh. It isn't at all unusual for me [or probably,
any patience game player] to get a PC this buff.
Notice the turn counter is over 40,000 already, a
point where many skilled players have already
ascended. I dawdle on all levels looking for good
equipment death drops, and this game has had maybe
_four_ bones levels so far (thank you, Hearse).

What _is_ unusual for me is getting much past this
stage of the game, buff or no. YASDs outweigh YAADs
by maybe 19::1 in that lifetime unachievement
record. It's been so long since I've seen Fort
Ludius I can barely remember the experience.

Sting and Demonbane were in the same bones pile, the
gauntlets of strength were in an armor shop, Frost
Brand was sitting randomly in an ordinary room on a
non-bones level, Vorpal Blade was a crowning gift.
The rest of the armor is pretty ordinary stuff,
patiently accumulated. The +3 helm and +1 speed
boots and +1 blue and green shield are "as found" in
normal dungeon grubbing, I think the cloak of
displacement was from the armor shop.  I've spent my
armor enchantment scrolls on getting the elven
mithral to +6. I've found, and stashed, 2
death-dropped +2 hard shoe pairs so far, they might
be worth polypiling for different equipment types
but I otherwise don't want them. I much prefer speed
from the speed boots to better armor class at this
point. [I've also gotten additional intrinsic speed
from a lucky encounter with a quantum mechanic, so
with the speed boots on I see even normally speedy
monsters granting me extra moves, and nasties like
lecrottas at least I'm fighting evenly matched.]

I was fortunate to loot a used book shop, and so ID
identify, enchant weapon, and enchant armor scrolls
early. That's a big part of the reason this is one
of my better games.

My healer has had 100% success so far writing
"blessed enchant weapon" and "blessed enchant armor"
scrolls using a blessed magic marker on blessed
blank scrolls. [With patience play, potions are as
common as popcorn, I don't even bother to identify
them in the early game unless I luck out with a mass
identify of my whole inventory, but instead create
dozens of potions of holy water and expend them
rapidly, thus the "belt and suspenders" blessing of
both magic marker and blank scrolls. In a stash
somewhere, I have two more (now cursed) magic
markers from two bones piles, waiting to be blessed
and recharged as needed.] Patient play in mines
yeilded the 20 daggers, and a smaller stash of four
or five elven daggers as well, kept in a chest
somewhere (I have four current stash chests, two of
them now emptied into the two atop altars) until I
get enough to be worth blessing and enchanting. I
need to consolidate my Mines Town altar-top stash
into my DLVL 9 altar-top stash, but laziness has so
far prevailed, so I've only made one round trip to
do that.

I probably forgot to mention that I'm altar camping
atop not only a stash-chest, but also a blessed
scroll of scare monster, which is very helpful. If I
find a wand of fire, and remember to do so, I plan
to paint Elbereth on many coin-decorated cells of
the small room where the altar is so that only
non-line of sight paths are available to approaching
monsters until melee range, which gives up my chance
to use daggers at range, but also avoids an
instadeath from some ranged attack against the PC.

The ring of conflict is exceptionally useful at
ridding myself of potential pets I don't care to
tame and risk leading off-level, but also don't want
clotting up the floor space where more useful
monsters could be coming into melee, and thus
sacrifice, range.

I'll try to let you know about the YASD as it
(almost inevitably) occurs.

